	    //@@@ The index returned by the indexof method is less than or equal to the count of array list object
	    Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && New_Ret <= New_arrListCount);
        }
    }    
}

ContractTest1.cs:

```csharp

[TestClass]
public class ArrayListContractTest 
{
    public void INDEXOF_CONTRACTTEST([PexMethod] void PUT_IndexOfContract([PexAssumeNotNull] ArrayList arrayList, int x) 
    {
        PUT_IndexOfContract(arrayList, x);
    }
}

```

This will generate a set of test cases around ArrayList's IndexOf method. In every test case, we have:
- Array list to be tested
- The element x we want to find the index in the array list

This test case will check if the array list's count remains the same before and after the IndexOf call. This test check for two conditions:
1. If x exists in the array, IndexOf method should return non-negative number and less than or equal to the count of the array list object
2. If IndexOf method returns any other number other than condition 1, it indicates that the behavior of the method had changed.

Please pay attention to the debug.assert function. If the condition in the debug.assert is not met, test case execution will halt. This provides quick feedback for the test case which broke.

In conclusion:

The Pex method generates exhaustive test cases and PexObserve is used for observation, PexAssumeNotNull is used to assume the pre-condition that array list should not be null. Debug.Assert is used for conditional run time assertions.

