A: Here are the Pex-test methods which ensure the ArrayList Add and Remove methods functionality and contract:

public void Test_AddContract([PexAssumeValid] object item)
{
   AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(item != null);

   ArrayList source = new ArrayList();
   ArrayList target = new ArrayList();

   if (target.Count != source.Count) 
   {
      PexObserve.ValueForViewing("source.Count", source.Count);
      PexObserve.ValueForViewing("target.Count", target.Count);
      Assert.Fail();
   }

   source.Add(item);
   target.Add(item);

   if (!IsEqualArrayList(source, target)) 
   {
      PexObserve.ValueForViewing("Failed source", source.ToArray());
      PexObserve.ValueForViewing("Failed target", target.ToArray());
      Assert.Fail();
   }

   CheckAddContract(source, item);
}

[PexMethod]
public void CheckAddContract(ArrayList list, object item) 
{
   AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(list != null); 

   int oldCount = list.Count;
   list.Add(item);
   int newCount = list.Count;

   int index = list.IndexOf(item);

   Assert.AreEqual(oldCount + 1, newCount);
   Assert.AreEqual(oldCount, index);
}

public void Test_RemoveContract(
   [PexAssumeValid] ArrayList source, 
   [PexAssumeValid] int index)
{
   AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index <= source.Count);
   
   ArrayList target = new ArrayList(source);

   if (index < 0 || index >= source.Count)
   {
      Assert.Throws<ArgumentOutOfRangeException>(() => target.RemoveAt(index));
      return;
   }

   object item = source[index];
   source.RemoveAt(index);
   target.RemoveAt(index);

   Assert.AreEqual(source.Count, target.Count);
   Assert.IsFalse(target.Contains(item));
}

public static bool IsEqualArrayList(ArrayList a1, ArrayList a2)
{
   if (a1.Count != a2.Count)
   {
      return false;
   }

   for (int i = 0; i < a1.Count; i++)
   {
      if (!object.Equals(a1[i], a2[i]))
      {
         return false;
      }
   }

   return true;
}

In these methods, we test the Add and Remove of ArrayList. Our tests are based on the assumption and observation in Pex. A unit test might check the number of elements in the array after adding, the added element will be in the array and at what position, also we are testing the remove method to check whether the element at specified index is removed from array and remaining elements in the array are correct.
We have used Assert class in C# for assertion operations. Fail function is used for indicating failing the test cases.

Please note these are only basic unit test cases, depending on the complexity of method, we might require a lot of unit test cases. Always--------------------	  Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount);

            // @@@ If the arraylist contains the value, returned index should be equal or greater than Old IndexOf  
 	    Assert.True(Old_arrListContainsX == New_arrListContainsX);
            
  	    Assert.True(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_Ret);	
        }

      [PexMethod]
      public void RunPUT_IndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest] ArrayList arrList, int x) => this.PUT_IndexOfContract(arrList, x);
    }
}

```

## Sample Output

1. In ArrayList.Test -> Test Method -> PUT_IndexOfContract you will see a series of asserts which will all be triggered and fail if the List.IndexOf has any sort of inconsistency.
2. You have an option to click through these failures and see a 'Value for Viewing' section - hovering over these will show you the values for each step in your test. 
   This will help you ensure that the values of `Old_arrListCount` and `New_arrListCount` are consistent throughout, check that if the array contains the item, `Old_arrListLastIndexOfX` <= `New_Ret`, check if the array contains the item `New_arrListContainsX` is True, and `Old_arrListContainsX` is the same as `New_arrListContainsX`.

Please note that this sample is an introduction and won't cover all possible edge cases when using ArrayList. You can extend these assertions or write more ones based on the specifics of you test scenario.

Also this example is very specific to `IndexOf` method. Other `ArrayList` methods - `Add`, `Remove`, `Insert`, etc. tests could be implemented similar way or in separate ones. 

Lastly, if your code base hasn't been set up for Pex testing yet, don't forget to set up your project to use Pex. The NuGet package - Microsoft.Pex (aka Pex) should be installed for pex testing to work correctly.

Also, Microsoft.Pex and Moles can be a bit tricky and requires a good understanding of their capabilities. The test should be the best guide to confirm the contract and functionality of the method being tested, and doesn't necessarily cover all possible inputs and edge cases.

For more advanced usage of Pex, you should refer to the official documentation, and tutorials available online. You can find them at - https://microsoft.github.io/art_of_test/

## Conclusion

PeEx is very powerful tool that can not only test all the methods of your application but also test edge cases of your methods and can even generate test input that covers your application code fully but this power comes with the responsibility to write correct assertions and test inputs. Always remember that each test case should be a single, focused unit of work.

Moreover, consider using a more advanced coverage tool like ReSharper. It provides the same functionality as PeEx but it doesn't require the Pex visual studio extension. It can assist you to generate test cases and code coverage reports, and even provides in-line suggestions to further improve your tests.

Happy Testing !

