A: The issue here is that Pex could be adding elements to the list, and this is where you're seeing different counts. If you want to ensure that the list remains unaffected, use the PexAssumeValidAttribute on the collection.
As for the IndexOf method, it only works when the collection has been populated during testing and the Add method is not run. 
Try running your tests again without this test case and see if the issue persists.
Another point to consider is that you're trying to call IndexOf on different types of collections, such as ArrayList and List<T>. ArrayList's IndexOf and List<T>'s IndexOf have different behaviors.
In general, the Pex tool may not fully understand the .NET Framework and therefore, could behave unpredictably, especially when using certain methods that have different behaviors depending on the type of the collection.
Hope this helped, let me know if you have any more questions.

A: The solution to this seems to be to just not use the ArrayList in the unit test. We have been using a wrapper class that encapsulates and abstracs the ArrayList logic and it works fine. 
I will start by suggesting using the wrapper class instead of using a raw ArrayList in the test case and see if the problem is resolved. 
Also, you may want to make sure your test cases are not manipulating the ArrayList data manually within the test case, especially if you're using a wrapper class, which means ArrayList data/behavior should directly depend on methods you've abstracted such as your wrapper class methods.
Let’s hope this works for your situation. 
Don't forget to mark your test cases with [PexMethod], and if needed, use [PexAssemblyUnderTest] with the assembly you want Pex to test. In Visual Studio 2010 there might not be visual feedback or coverage analysis, but the same logic applies to using Pex with other .NET languages.
Hope this helps,
Cheers,
