Note: This contract test is for the ArrayList.IndexOf Method. In every situation that you call IndexOf, either by passing a parameter value or by using a valid state, we should be able to call IndexOf again on the state and have a result other than -1. 

If the return value is non-negative, list counts of new and old array are equal. This is because the method documentation in C# states that "This method returns the position of the first occurrence of an item in a list. If item is not found in the list, the method returns -1." 

If the return value is non-negative, new index matches last index of the list. This is because once the first occurrence of the item is found, the next IndexOf will start from the next item. 

If x is in the array, contains should return true on new array. This is because if you found the first occurrence of the item, then it's in the array. 

Note: This contract test might not cover all the cases. You should add more in order to cover all the potential edge cases. Also, this test assumes that the list already contains x. If this is not the case, the contract may fail. 

Remember to add the actual contract test to the test explorer.

You can run this with nunit test console runner or with Visual Studio 2010's test framework. If you include the ContractTestFixture.cs(this is a class that holds your contract tests) in your test project, it should run correctly.

You can check out the Microsoft documentation on how to perform arraylist contract testing. It's located here: http://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/cose/features/contracts/contracts.aspx
Hope it helps and good luck with your testing :)

A: Pex is an automated equivalence partitioning toolkit. It's designed to help developers write unit tests more efficiently. PEX is not a silver bullet for unit test coverage. It does help in giving a broad range of different inputs to unit tests, and in helping determine when your unit tests are incorrect (i.e. when some class behavior is not captured by your tests).
Generally when you consider Pex in conjunction with other testing types, including unit tests and integration tests (specifically, functional or behavioral testing), you can often generate more comprehensive coverage that a purely Pex-only approach can offer.
That having been said, you should use Pex with other testing practices you have already. It just gives a more exhaustive set of possible inputs for your tests each time you run Pex. While Pex can help you generate a large number of test cases, it still requires human judgment to decide which of those tests are likely to fail, and you should still manually write tests for boundary cases, exception scenarios, and complex interactions in a way that's meaningful to your application.
In summary: Use Pex for generating a large set of different kinds of test cases, but then conduct manual testing for edge-case scenarios and complex interactions after Pex tests.

A: Also, ArrayList may not always be used to test Equivalence classes as they might not perform as expected. In general, arrays should be preferred for testing purposes due to simpler behaviors and easier properties to check (like order of the elements, length, duplicates etc).  
For a good understanding of why and how to use ArrayList you need to study its behaviors, how it will perform, and what properties it should have according to software requirement specifications (SRSS) and business needs.
Also, you may also consider the other class types if they suit your specific testing needs better. For example, LinkedList, HashSet and HashMap. 

